<Slide style={{ width: 1280, height: 720, background: '#0A1020', fontFamily: 'Arial', padding: '52px 60px 30px 60px', display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column' }}>
    <Text style={{ fontSize: 12, color: '#4D8DFF', fontWeight: 'bold', letterSpacing: 2.2 }}>07 · RESULTS (3/6) — H1, RE-TESTED</Text>
    <Text style={{ fontSize: 32, fontWeight: 'bold', color: '#FFFFFF', lineHeight: 1.2, marginTop: 8 }}>
        Matching the data volume does not rescue H1
    </Text>
    <Box style={{ width: 1160, height: 1, background: '#24334C', marginTop: 14 }} />

    <Text style={{ fontSize: 14, color: '#A8B8CE', marginTop: 18, lineHeight: 1.6 }}>
        Tier 1 holds 8,055 and 3,796 rows; tier 2 holds 1,034 and 711. To separate tier from sample size, tier 1 was downsampled to
        tier 2's actual training counts — <span style={{ color: '#FFFFFF' }}>497 and 723 rows, not the row totals</span> — and both arms were retrained, test rows untouched.
    </Text>

    <Table
        style={{ width: 1160, height: 196, marginTop: 18 }}
        defaultTextStyle={{ fontSize: 14, textAlign: 'right', color: '#C3D0E2' }}
        defaultCellStyle={{ border: { bottom: { width: 1, color: '#1D2A40' } }, background: { color: '#0E1728' } }}
        cells={[
            [
                { text: 'Matched n', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FFFFFF', fontSize: 12, textAlign: 'left' }, cellStyle: { background: { color: '#16233A' } } },
                { text: 'Λ, tier 1', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FFFFFF', fontSize: 12 }, cellStyle: { background: { color: '#16233A' } } },
                { text: 'Λ, tier 2', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FFFFFF', fontSize: 12 }, cellStyle: { background: { color: '#16233A' } } },
                { text: 'Δ = tier1 − tier2', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FFFFFF', fontSize: 12 }, cellStyle: { background: { color: '#16233A' } } },
                { text: '95% interval', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FFFFFF', fontSize: 12 }, cellStyle: { background: { color: '#16233A' } } },
            ],
            [
                { text: '497 rows', textStyle: { textAlign: 'left', color: '#FFFFFF' } },
                '−0.041', '−0.008',
                { text: '−0.033', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FF6B6B' } },
                '[−0.042, −0.024]',
            ],
            [
                { text: '723 rows', textStyle: { textAlign: 'left', color: '#FFFFFF' } },
                '−0.104', '−0.016',
                { text: '−0.087', textStyle: { bold: true, color: '#FF6B6B' } },
                '[−0.153, −0.022]',
            ],
            [
                { text: 'full data (reference)', textStyle: { textAlign: 'left', color: '#8FA3BC' } },
                { text: '−0.120', textStyle: { color: '#8FA3BC' } },
                { text: '−0.020', textStyle: { color: '#8FA3BC' } },
                { text: '−0.100', textStyle: { color: '#8FA3BC' } },
                { text: '—', textStyle: { color: '#8FA3BC' } },
            ],
        ]}
    />

    <Box style={{ flexDirection: 'row', gap: 40, marginTop: 24 }}>
        <Box style={{ flex: 1, flexDirection: 'column', gap: 8 }}>
            <Text style={{ fontSize: 15, color: '#FFFFFF', fontWeight: 'bold', lineHeight: 1.45 }}>
                Tier 1 stays below tier 2 at equal n, and both intervals exclude zero.
            </Text>
            <Text style={{ fontSize: 13, color: '#A8B8CE', lineHeight: 1.65 }}>
                The direction matches H1. So the tier/sample-size confound is not what flattens ρ to zero — it explains part of the
                magnitude (full −0.100 shrinks to −0.033 at 497 rows) but not the sign or the collapse.
            </Text>
        </Box>
        <Box style={{ flex: 1, flexDirection: 'column', gap: 8 }}>
            <Text style={{ fontSize: 15, color: '#FFB020', fontWeight: 'bold', lineHeight: 1.45 }}>
                The break is inside tier 3: +0.068 versus −1.479.
            </Text>
            <Text style={{ fontSize: 13, color: '#A8B8CE', lineHeight: 1.65 }}>
                Two tasks in the same tier sit at the top and the bottom of the ranking, so the rank correlation snaps there. "One point
                drives it" and "sample size confounds it" are now two separate statements: tier 1 vs tier 2 is stable, tier 3 is undecidable.
            </Text>
        </Box>
    </Box>

    <Box style={{ marginTop: 'auto', flexDirection: 'row', justifyContent: 'space-between' }}>
        <Text style={{ fontSize: 12, color: '#5E7590', width: 900, lineHeight: 1.5 }}>
            Limits: A4 refuses n-levels by design, so this row exists for A3 only; on tier 2 the graph arm loses rows (723 → 651, 497 → 447),
            which biases Δ toward zero — the negative Δ observed is conservative.
        </Text>
        <Text style={{ fontSize: 12, color: '#5E7590' }}>10 / 17</Text>
    </Box>
</Slide>
